course: dictionary
lesson: 13869
title: Keenly: eager, strong and sharp
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Keenly is an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done. It often sits next to a verb. «Mia keenly awaited her birthday party» means Mia waited with great eagerness, because keenly tells you how she waited.
explain: Keenly can mean three things, and the context tells you which one. «The fans keenly awaited the new book» shows eagerness. «She keenly felt the loss of her old dog» shows strong, deep feeling. «The referee watched keenly for any foul» shows sharp, careful attention.
explain: To check your meaning, put it into the sentence in place of keenly. If the sentence still makes sense with eagerly, strongly or carefully, you have found the right meaning. Writers use keenly in stories and reports to show that feelings or attention are strong.
fact: Keenly is an adverb, so it tells you how someone does something.
fact: «Keenly» means eagerly, with a lot of interest, as in «keenly interested».
fact: «Keenly» can mean very strongly or deeply, as in «keenly felt».
fact: «Keenly» can mean sharply and carefully, as in «watched keenly».
fact: Keen is an adjective, as in «a keen player», and keenly is the adverb form.
exercise: «Sam keenly awaited the school trip.» What does keenly mean here? | Sadly, with a long face | Quietly, under his breath | *Eagerly, with lots of excitement | Slowly, one step at a time
exercise: «She keenly felt the loss of her old dog.» What does keenly mean here? | Loudly and proudly | Only a little bit | Carelessly, without thinking | *Very strongly and deeply
exercise: «The cat watched keenly as the mouse crept out.» Find and copy the word that tells you how the cat watched. | out | mouse | *keenly | cat
test: What does keenly mean in «The fans keenly awaited the new film»? | Sadly and slowly | Angrily | *Eagerly, with great interest | Rudely
test: Which sentence uses keenly to mean very strongly? | The bus was late again | The rain fell on the roof | Tom ate his lunch quickly | *Grandma keenly missed her home town after she moved
exam: Ella says keenly means slowly. Is she right? | Yes, because keenly sounds like keen | No, keenly means loudly | *No, keenly can mean eagerly, strongly or carefully, but not slowly | Yes, keenly always means slowly
